%AAn\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Kung Tz\u016d-ch\u00ean\">Kung Tz\u016d-ch\u00ean</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gong_Dingzi\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Gong Dingzi\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367335\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367335\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3642426</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Kung Ting-tz\u016d</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"431\" data-page-number=\"431\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/443\" data-page-index=\"443\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/443\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;443\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>KUNG</b> Ting-tz\u016d \u9f94\u9f0e\u5b73 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u5b5d\u5347,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u829d\u9e93), Jan. 6, 1616\u20131673, Oct. 21, poet, landscape painter and official under three r\u00e9gimes in Peking during the Ming-Ch'ing transition period, was a native of Ho-fei, Anhwei. After becoming a <i>chin-shih</i> in 1634 at the early age of twenty <i>sui</i>, he served as magistrate of Ch'i-shui, Hupeh, for six or seven years (1635\u2013?) and was then promoted to be a censor in Peking. When Peking fell into the hands of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Tz%C5%AD-ch%27%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch&#39;\u00eang\">Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] for a brief period in 1644, Kung was given the concurrent post of superintendent of police of the north city. Under the Manchus he continued to serve as a censor and in other capacities. In later years he served as president of the Board of Punishments (1664\u201366), of the Board of War (1666\u201369), and of the Board of Ceremonies (1669\u201373). In 1670, and again in 1673, he was chief examiner in the metropolitan examinations. The posthumous title Tuan-i \u7aef\u6bc5 was conferred on him, but was revoked by order of Emperor Kao-tsung in 1769.\n</p><p>Kung Ting-tz\u016d was well known in his time as a poet, and it is as a poet that he is likely to be remembered by posterity. His name is linked with that of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ch%27ien_Ch%27ien-i\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ch&#39;ien Ch&#39;ien-i\">Ch'ien Ch'ien-i</a></span> and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wu_Wei-yeh\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wu Wei-yeh\">Wu Wei-yeh</a></span>&#32;[<i>qq.&#8239;v.</i>] as one of the \"three master poets of the eastern Yangtze region\" (\u6c5f\u5de6\u4e09\u5927\u5bb6). His collected poems, \u5b9a\u5c71\u5802\u96c6 <i>Ting-shan-t'ang chi</i>, were first printed in 1673, but were included in the list of banned books in the Ch'ien-lung reign-period. However, a fuller edition in 43 <i>ch\u00fcan</i> with 4 supplementary <i>ch\u00fcan</i> of poetry in irregular meter (here called <i>shih-y\u00fc</i> \u8a69\u9918, but usually known as <i>tz'\u016d</i> \u8a5e) was printed with the same title in 1883 by a descendant in the fourteenth generation. This descendant also printed his ancestor's memorials to the throne, \u9f94\u7aef\u6bc5\u516c\u594f\u758f <i>Kung Tuan-i kung tsou-su</i>, in 8 <i>ch\u00fcan</i> with 2 appendices. Kung Ting-tz\u016d had a concubine by the name of Ku Mei \u9867\u7709 (\u6e44, \u5d4b or \u5a9a, T. \u7709[\u6885]\u751f or \u7709\u838a H. \u6a6b\u6ce2) whose family name was originally Hs\u00fc \u5f90. She was talented in music, poetry, and painting, excelling particularly in the delineation of orchids and bamboo.\n</p><p>For his relations with Adam Schall see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yang_Kuang-hsien\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yang Kuang-hsien\">Yang Kuang-hsien</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>].\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/489/17a; 6/44/11a; 19/\u7532\u4e0a/14b; <i>Ho-fei-hsien chih</i> (1920 reprint of 1803 ed.) 24/1b; Li Chia-fu \u674e\u5bb6\u5b5a, <i>Ho-fei shih-hua</i> (\u8a69\u8a71, 1928), \u4e0a/1a; L.T.C.L.H.M., 465b lists 3 paintings by Ku Mei: 6 others on orchids and bamboo were reproduced by the \u6709\u6b63\u66f8\u5c40 <i>Yu-ch\u00eang shu-ch\u00fc</i>, Shanghai, as supplement 39 in the series \u4e2d\u570b\u540d\u756b\u96c6 <i>Chung-kuo ming-hua chi</i>; Goodrich, L. C., <i>The Literary Inquisition of Ch'ien-lung</i>, p. 220; <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Y%C3%BC_Huai\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Y\u00fc Huai\">Y\u00fc Huai</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], <i>Pan-ch'iao tsa-chi</i>, <i>ch\u00fcan</i> \u4e2d.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ch'ien Ch'ien-i"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Kung, Prince"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Kung Tz\u016d-ch\u00ean"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wu Wei-yeh"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yang Kuang-hsien"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Y\u00fc Huai"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Tu Lien-ch\u00ea"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = kung ting-tzu\n | previous = Kung, Prince\n | next     = Kung Tz\u016d-ch\u00ean\n | from     = 443\n | to       = 443\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Tu Lien-ch\u00ea\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Kung Ting-tz\u016d}}"}}}